  "content": "# Why Database Indexes Speed Up Reads but Slow Down Writes\n\n## The Core Idea\n\nAn index is a **separate data structure** (usually a B-tree) that maps indexed column values to the locations of the corresponding rows. It's like the index at the back of a textbook: instead of scanning every page to find a topic, you look it up alphabetically and jump straight to the right page.\n\nThat extra structure is what makes reads fast — and it's also what makes writes slow.\n\n---\n\n## Why Reads Get Faster\n\nWithout an index, a query like `SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'a@b.com'` requires a **full table scan**: the database reads every row and checks the condition. That's O(n).\n\nWith an index on `email`, the database walks a B-tree in O(log n) steps and lands directly on the matching row(s). For a table with millions of rows, that's the difference between seconds and milliseconds.\n\n---\n\n## Why Writes Get Slower\n\nEvery `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, or `DELETE` must keep **both the table and every index on it** consistent. So a single write becomes multiple writes:\n\n- **INSERT** → add the row to the table *and* insert a",
